{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["And the reason for <b>you shall not deliver a slave to his master</b>: it is attached to what precedes it, that if the slave flees from his master — the camp against which you marched — and escapes to your camp, you shall not deliver him back to him for money that he may give you. And since it says \"in the place that he shall choose,\" it appears that this is a commandment that he be a free man, and we shall not enslave him. And the reason for this commandment is that he will serve HaShem with us, and it is not fitting that we return him to his master to serve idolatry. Furthermore, it is possible that he may teach the way of access to the city, for in such a manner many cities are captured by means of slaves and captives who flee from there. And our Rabbis said (Gittin 45a): even a Canaanite slave of an Israelite who fled from outside the Land to the Land — for this one too will stand before the inhabitants of the Land of HaShem and be saved from serving those who dwell on impure soil, and because not all the commandments apply there."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "I read השם in the phrase יושבי ארץ השם as HaShem ('the Land of HaShem'), following the standard idiom; the following clause 'those who dwell on impure soil' contrasts dwellers in the Holy Land with those outside it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:04.833166873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}